#: ../../compatibility.rst:68
msgid "``rst_prolog`` follows leading bibliographic fields. ``rst_epilog`` follows the source. Neither is inserted into Markdown. ``only`` and ``ifconfig`` keep their sections when the condition holds. Their placement can differ from Sphinx when a conditional section changes the surrounding section level. Section titles inside MyST ``eval-rst`` are errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:74
msgid "References and domains"
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:77
msgid "Guidedog implements toctrees, labels, glossaries, numbered sections and figures, ``ref``, ``doc``, ``numref``, ``term``, ``download``, ``any``, ``keyword``, ``option``, ``envvar``, and ``token`` references. Objects can appear in toctrees, sidebars, and the page outline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:82
msgid "The standard, Python, C, C++, JavaScript, reStructuredText, and math domains are implemented. The Odin and Rust domains are Guidedog's own extensions. They describe packages or crates, declarations, signatures, and generated API pages. See :doc:`odin` and :doc:`rust` for their source-only discovery and limits."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:87
msgid "Parameter, return, exception, and variable fields form structured descriptions. Canonical names become aliases for references and inventories. Python defaults and annotations retain their source spelling. Sphinx may print them again through Python's unparser. C++ declarations publish Sphinx-compatible identifier versions and symbol inventories. Nesting limits still apply. Nested parentheses are parsed in linear time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:94
msgid "``numref`` substitutes one number slot and reports an unnumbered target. Section numbering follows reading order. A document is numbered once. A second numbered toctree reports the conflict. See the reference tests for format substitution and identifier details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:99
msgid "The general index, module index, and search are built in. Search matches word prefixes. It does not copy Sphinx's English stemming."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:102
msgid "Declarative object types replace a useful class of Python extension registrations. ``object_types``, ``crossref_types``, and ``directive_aliases`` describe their data. A custom Python ``parse_node`` becomes declarative name, display, and program rules. Migration reports code it cannot express. See :doc:`object-types`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:107
#: ../../compatibility.rst:112
msgid "Built-in extensions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:110
msgid "Every extension in Sphinx's ``sphinx.ext`` package is built in. A project lists it by its Sphinx name in ``extensions``, and its directives, roles, settings and builders are there. The settings keep Sphinx's names and defaults, so ``guidedog migrate`` carries them over from ``conf.py``. Where Sphinx imports Python, Guidedog reads the source instead. Where Sphinx calls a program, Guidedog uses what it has built in. GDS 0011 records the design of each one."
msgstr ""

