#: ../../compatibility.rst:151
msgid "Reading source has limits that importing does not. Compiled modules, objects created at run time, external decorators, computed values and extension event hooks cannot be seen. Python 3.13 and the pinned Sphinx documenter tests provide the reference behavior. See :doc:`autodoc` for the full settings and exceptions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:153
msgid "A third-party extension, such as ``sphinx_design``, is not built in. Configuration that names one is refused, and ``guidedog migrate`` leaves it out with a note. An unknown directive or role is reported at its source location. **Its omitted content must be reviewed before publication.** Declared object types (:doc:`object-types`) and build plugins (:doc:`plugins`) cover many of them. Other theme names are accepted using Guidedog's theme implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:158
msgid "Trust and resource limits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:161
msgid "A normal build accepts the project's raw HTML and Typst, templates, and network inventories. Local reads stay within the source directory and explicitly shared roots. A symlink does not grant access outside that boundary. Templates, static files, includes, images, fonts, and API sources obey the same rule."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:166
msgid "Graphviz image resources are resolved relative to their document and confined. Supported image bytes are embedded in the drawing. ``imagepath`` and ``fontpath`` do not expand the boundary. Typst runs with a private root containing the readable folders. This keeps book templates and preambles within the declared resources."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:171
msgid "``--untrusted`` narrows the read boundary to the source folder. It omits raw content, unsafe URLs, outside resources, project Typst templates, and preambles, with diagnostics. It fetches no inventories or Typst packages. Graphs that ask to read resources are shown as code with a warning. This mode does not bound the native compiler's memory or execution time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:177
msgid "The host budget defaults to 1 GiB. It is coarse: it counts the sources, included files, and PDF output the host holds, reserved before they are read. Each worker reuses one workspace. The retained project graph and catalogs still consume memory as the project grows. Reading workers coordinate admission. A refused request reports the failing operation and a budget that fits. ``GUIDEDOG_AVAILABLE_MIB`` can supply known machine headroom. Working memory always stays in memory. There is no disk-backed mode, and ``--memory=disk`` and ``--disk-budget`` are refused."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:184
msgid "``--max-depth``, ``--max-nodes``, and ``--stack-kib`` bound document work. Changing a limit invalidates cached reading and output. Depth and stack capacity must agree. Native Graphviz, tree-sitter, and Typst allocations are outside the host budget. We use operating-system limits when those need a hard bound. See :doc:`errors` and :doc:`internals/memory`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:190
msgid "Verification and real projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:193
msgid "``cargo xtask sphinxdiff-roots`` compares Guidedog with a pinned Sphinx 9.1.0 over 42 of Sphinx's own test roots. It checks pages, identifiers, body links, inventories, numbering, and diagnostics. The stored results explain each difference, for example precise source locations, stable reading-order numbering, and explicit reports for omitted target-specific raw content (:doc:`internals/sphinx-comparison`). The reader conformance tests (:doc:`internals/rst-conformance`, :doc:`internals/commonmark-conformance`) keep their expected outputs, and ``cargo xtask verify-docutils`` checks those against a pinned Docutils 0.22.4. All 98 trees match."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:195
msgid "On 15 October 2026 we built the manuals of CPython, Django and Flask as HTML and as PDF books, clean and unchanged (:doc:`evidence/manuals`). Every HTML build succeeded, and the link check found no broken internal link besides two inert ones in CPython. The Django and Flask books built; the CPython book needs more memory than the 12 GB machine had."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:197
msgid "Those projects contain unsupported Python extension constructs. Their diagnostics remain part of the evidence. A zero exit status does not mean all their private directives were reproduced. We run with ``-W`` when publishing requires zero diagnostics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../compatibility.rst:199
msgid "The measurements describe the recorded workloads on the recorded machine. They are not universal speed or memory bounds."
msgstr ""
